# potluck-head workers file. One worker per line:
#   "host:port [weight] [ram_mb] [vram_mb]"
#
# host:port  -- where potluck-worker listens on that machine. On each host, start
#              the worker manually with that machine's own address, e.g.:
#                ./potluck-worker models/Qwen3.8-27B-Q4_K_M.gguf 192.168.1.20 40000
# weight     -- optional integer capability score, default 1. The head splits
#              the model's layers proportionally to the weights (2 = ~twice the
#              layers). --profile overwrites these with measured throughput.
# ram_mb     -- optional per-machine RAM budget in MiB for THIS machine's layer
#              weights + KV cache. Only enforced by --lp (HiGHS LP scheduler):
#              the solver may give the machine at most floor(budget / bytes
#              per layer) layers. Absent = unlimited.
# vram_mb    -- optional per-machine GPU memory budget in MiB for offloaded
#              layer weights. 0 forces the machine CPU-only. Only enforced by
#              --lp, which also caps that worker's -ngl at the budget.
#
# Both budgets are positional: to set vram_mb without ram_mb, write a generous
# ram_mb first. Lines starting with '#' are ignored. Order matters: layer
# window w belongs to line w, and hidden states flow from line 0 toward the
# last line.

# M4 laptop, local Metal worker (Mac): 16 GB unified, fast GPU.
192.168.1.66:40000 3 16384 12288

# PC with GTX 1650 SUPER (Arch Linux): only 4 GB VRAM -> keep the GPU budget
# honest so --lp never offloads more than it can hold. CPU layers go to RAM.
192.168.1.78:40001 2 12288 3584

# pink M1 Air, remote Metal worker (16 GB unified).
192.168.1.21:40002 1 12288 12288

# headless Intel MBP (Ubuntu), CPU-only: vram_mb 0 keeps it offload-free.
# Weights must be >= 1; omit the line entirely to leave the machine out.
#192.168.1.60:40003 1 8192 0
